# Haryana Economy: Agriculture, Livestock, Industry and Transport

> Haryana feeds the country and builds its cars. A small state of about
> 1.4 per cent of India's area, it was a Green Revolution leader, supplies
> a large share of the central foodgrain pool and hosts the largest
> automobile cluster in the country around Gurugram and Manesar. Exam
> questions ask the farm institutes, the livestock breeds, the industrial
> town to product pairs and the main highways, so learn them as pairs.

## 1. The Big Picture (figures with years)

| Indicator | Figure | Source and year |
| --- | --- | --- |
| GSDP, current prices | About 12.14 lakh crore rupees | Economic Survey of Haryana 2024-25, advance estimate for 2024-25 |
| GSDP growth, constant prices | 7.6 per cent | same |
| Per capita income | 3,53,182 rupees at current prices (India: 2,05,579) | same |
| Sector shares of GSVA | Services 51.2%, Industry 32.9%, Agriculture and allied 15.9% | same, constant 2011-12 prices |

Haryana's per capita income is far above the national average, which is
why papers describe it as one of the richest large states. Rank questions
change with the year and the data source, so rely on the figure and the
"above national average" fact, not on a rank you cannot date.

[definition]
**GSDP** is Gross State Domestic Product, the value of all goods and
services produced within the state in a year. **GSVA** is gross value
added, which strips out net taxes and is used to show sector shares.
[/definition]

## 2. Agriculture and the Green Revolution

The Green Revolution of the 1960s, the completion of the Bhakra Dam in 1963
and the Western Yamuna Command canal network of the 1970s lifted foodgrain
output sharply. Haryana became self-sufficient in food and is among the
largest contributors, after Punjab, to the central foodgrain pool of wheat
and rice. It supplies about 60 per cent of India's basmati rice exports.
Wheat and rice are the main crops, with the plains growing wheat in rabi
and paddy in kharif.

| Season | Main crops of Haryana |
| --- | --- |
| Kharif | Rice (including basmati), cotton, bajra, sugarcane, maize |
| Rabi | Wheat, mustard (rapeseed), barley, gram |

Cotton belongs to the southwest (Sirsa, Fatehabad, Hisar), bajra to the
dry south and west (Bhiwani, Mahendragarh), and basmati rice to Karnal,
Kaithal and Kurukshetra. Roughly three-quarters of the cultivated area is
irrigated, mostly by canals and tube-wells.

## 3. Livestock, Dairy and Research Institutes

Haryana is famous for dairy. The **Murrah** buffalo, a high-yielding
milch breed, is native to the state's Rohtak-Hisar-Jind belt, and the
**Hariana** (Haryana) cattle breed is a dual-purpose breed from the same
region. Milk availability per head is among the highest in India.

| Institution | Place | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CCS Haryana Agricultural University (CCS HAU) | Hisar | Founded 1970 as Haryana Agricultural University; renamed after Chaudhary Charan Singh in 1991 |
| Lala Lajpat Rai University of Veterinary and Animal Sciences (LUVAS) | Hisar | Formed in 2010 by separating the veterinary colleges from HAU |
| Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes (CIRB) | Hisar | ICAR institute for buffalo research |
| National Research Centre on Equines | Hisar | Horse, donkey and mule research |
| National Dairy Research Institute (NDRI) | Karnal | Shifted to Karnal in 1955; deemed university since 1989 |
| Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research | Karnal | ICAR wheat research body |

Hisar is therefore the "agri-livestock capital", and Karnal the
"dairy-wheat research capital". A common exam confusion is to place NDRI
at Hisar or CIRB at Karnal, so fix the pair firmly: NDRI is Karnal, CIRB is Hisar.

## 4. Industry and Industrial Towns

Haryana is the largest automobile hub in the country and a leading base
for IT and business services around Gurugram. State industrial estates and
industrial model townships (IMTs) are developed by **HSIIDC**, set up on
8 March 1967 (as HSIDC; renamed HSIIDC in 2005), headquartered at Panchkula.

| Town or cluster | Known for |
| --- | --- |
| Gurugram | Corporate offices, IT and business services, financial centre |
| Manesar and Gurugram | Automobiles, such as Maruti Suzuki, and component makers |
| Faridabad | Large and medium engineering industries, including tractors and tyres |
| Panipat | Handloom textiles, blankets, carpets, and the Indian Oil refinery |
| Yamunanagar | Paper, plywood, sugar, and heavy engineering |
| Sonipat (Kundli) | Industrial belt on the Delhi border; food processing and engineering |
| Rohtak and Bahadurgarh (Jhajjar) | Footwear, small industry and engineering clusters |
| Rewari | Light industry and auto-parts clusters |

[example]
Question: "Panipat is known as the city of weavers. Which large petroleum
facility is also located there?" Answer: the Panipat Refinery of Indian
Oil Corporation, described as one of the largest in South Asia.
[/example]

Haryana also operates several Special Economic Zones, mainly along the
Delhi corridor. The state's exports are strongest in basmati rice,
automobiles and textiles.

## 5. Transport and Connectivity

| Mode | Key facts |
| --- | --- |
| Roads | About 26,000 km of roads, including roughly 2,500 km of national highways (older survey data) |
| Grand Trunk Road | The old Sher Shah Suri road passes through Ambala, Kurukshetra, Karnal, Panipat and Sonipat; now part of NH-44 |
| Delhi-Jaipur route | NH-48 through Gurugram, Manesar and Rewari |
| Expressways | **KMP (Kundli-Manesar-Palwal) Expressway**, a ring of about 135 km around Delhi; Delhi-Gurugram Expressway; Delhi-Mumbai Expressway also touches Haryana |
| Railways | Served by the Northern, North Western and North Central zones; Ambala Cantonment, Kurukshetra and Rewari are well-known junctions |
| Air | Hisar is the planned site of an aerocity and aviation hub; the nearest large airports are Delhi and Chandigarh |
| State buses | Haryana Roadways runs one of the largest state fleets, with several thousand buses |

## 6. Exam Traps

- NDRI is at Karnal, CIRB and CCS HAU are at Hisar.
- Murrah is a buffalo; Hariana is a cattle breed.
- Panipat for handlooms and refinery; Yamunanagar for paper and plywood.
- HSIIDC is in Panchkula and was set up in 1967.
- Always attach a year to per capita income and GSDP figures.

[summary]
Haryana's Green Revolution made it a leading contributor to the central
foodgrain pool and the source of about 60 per cent of basmati exports. The
Murrah buffalo and Hariana cattle underpin dairy, with research at Hisar
(CCS HAU, CIRB) and Karnal (NDRI). Industry is led by Gurugram and Manesar
(IT and automobiles), Faridabad (engineering), Panipat (textiles and
refinery) and Yamunanagar (paper). In 2024-25 per capita income was about
3.53 lakh rupees against India's 2.06 lakh. HSIIDC, set up in 1967, builds
industrial estates, while the KMP Expressway and the GT Road corridor
carry traffic.
[/summary]
